{"kind":"request","text":"Codex: revive the salvageable part of the 500k follow-up. This answers your c6ecf3e5 with the first of its two outcomes — a fresh current-world request — but only for three of the five files, and with one condition I care about more than the memory saving.\n\nWHAT I FOUND, by reading the diff and testing it rather than by reading its description. The worktree at request/resident-memory-500k has HEAD adda28c8, which is an ancestor of main, so the committed work genuinely landed and only the uncommitted changes are in question. Those are five files, +235/-15.\n\nThree of the five apply cleanly to current main b87afa1e and pass there. I applied exactly those three in a scratch worktree cut from main: go vet ./... exit 0, go test ./... -count=1 exit 0 with 27 packages ok, 0 FAIL and 1 with no test files, and internal/kernel green in 90 s. So this is not a speculative revival — the salvage is verified working at today's main, and I have preserved the exact patch at /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-worktrees/500k-followup-salvage.patch so nothing depends on that worktree surviving.\n\nTwo of the five do not apply. internal/workroom/fold.go and fold_test.go fail against current main because fold.go has moved substantially under the merge-driven succession work. That part is the string interning — a per-Folder map canonicalizing repeated actors, schemas, rests_on entries, kinds, text and body fields, on the observation that a durable history repeats a small vocabulary and JSON decoding gives every occurrence its own backing bytes. The idea is still sound and is independent of the mechanics that moved, but it must be re-implemented against today's fold.go rather than salvaged. Do not try to force the old diff.\n\nWHAT THE TWO SALVAGEABLE PARTS DO, so the review has something to check against. appendBorrowedChunk encodes complete chunks while the verified event slice is still owned by the reader, borrowing immutable payload bytes instead of cloning every historical payload merely to compress and discard the clone; only the bounded tail survives the call and needs independent ownership. The schema.go change replaces a json.Decoder over a bytes.Reader with json.Unmarshal, on the ground that the Decoder's refill buffer copies every payload and makes cold projection memory scale with all bytes read, and replaces Marshal-then-compare with a streaming comparison writer to avoid a full-size output copy per payload.\n\nTHE CONDITION I CARE ABOUT. The schema.go change is not merely an optimization: it edits the canonicality gate that decides which payloads the workroom will admit. It deletes DisallowUnknownFields and the decoder.More() check and argues in a comment that the byte-for-byte canonical comparison subsumes both, because re-encoding cannot equal input containing anything the typed value did not consume, and because Unmarshal rejects trailing JSON values.\n\nI traced that argument and I believe it is correct — an unknown field is dropped by the typed value and so cannot survive re-encoding, and trailing whitespace fails the exact-length check. But \"I believe it is correct\" is precisely the standard that should not carry a change to an admission boundary. Condition 2 requires adversarial tests that make the argument load-bearing rather than rhetorical, each failing if the comparison is weakened. If those tests turn out to be hard to write, that is information: it would mean the property is less crisp than the comment claims, and the safer answer is to keep the explicit checks and take only the borrowed-chunk half.\n\nAND A CONDITION ON THE WHOLE THING. Measure it. Both parts are memory optimizations justified by reasoning about copies, and neither carries a number. performance/ exists precisely so this can be answered; condition 5 asks for the reduction each part actually delivers at a named depth on named hardware. Anything that does not measurably help should be dropped rather than landed on the strength of a plausible story about allocations — that is how a codebase accretes complexity that nobody can later justify removing.\n\nI have not judged whether this still serves the FIRST-PRODUCTION memory goal, because I do not have the current target to judge against; cfca4477 is still open and its supported-envelope table is exactly the thing that would make that judgement possible. Condition 5 is my substitute: rather than assert relevance, require the number that would demonstrate it.","body":{"conditions":"1. Land the two salvageable memory reductions on current main: the borrowed-chunk checkpoint compression in internal/kernel/checkpoint.go with its kernel_test.go coverage, and the decode/canonicality change in internal/workroom/schema.go. A verified patch of exactly these three files is preserved at /Users/hughpyle/play/gitseq-worktrees/500k-followup-salvage.patch. 2. SECURITY CONDITION, the one that matters: the schema.go change removes DisallowUnknownFields and the decoder.More() check, arguing that the byte-for-byte canonical comparison subsumes both. That argument must be defended by adversarial tests, not by the comment alone. Cover at minimum: an unknown field, a duplicate key, trailing whitespace, trailing JSON values, a non-canonical numeric or escape encoding, and empty input. Each must still be refused, and each test must fail if the canonical comparison is weakened. 3. The borrowed-chunk path must not retain any reference to reader-owned bytes beyond the call; state plainly which slices are borrowed and which are owned, and cover the tail-boundary case where a partial tail precedes a complete chunk. 4. The string interning in internal/workroom/fold.go is NOT salvageable and must be re-implemented, not carried: that file has moved under the merge-driven succession work and the old diff no longer applies. Re-implement only if it still measures; see the measurement condition below. 5. Measure, do not assume. Report the memory reduction each part actually delivers against a named scenario in performance/, at a named depth, on named hardware. A memory optimization with no measurement is a complexity increase. Drop any part that does not measurably help. 6. make vet, make test, make race, make build, gofmt and diff-check pass at the exact head; artifacts at every maintained path the head changes; independent Architecture/Security/Simplification review before merge.","priority":"P2","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123","verified_at":"main b87afa1e on 2026-08-21"}}